#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bd2d24-7148-5ea0-938f-3c79b0f3f1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7c99605a-2d55-5d37-b363-2e33fa02575c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10456" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 - \\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{y} &= 1 - x \\\\\n\\sqrt{y} &= 3(1 - x) \\\\\n(\\sqrt{y})^{2} &= (3(1 - x))^{2} \\\\\ny &= 9(1 - x)^{2} \\\\\ny &= 9(x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 9(x - 1)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\leq 1, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65cc2d11-c785-538a-a64d-5d3349040676" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Radical Function Containing a Linear Argument"}] :question/id #uuid "8f01f2b7-13d5-58d3-8e2e-9f6804993094" :question/math-academy-id "e-10457" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4x + 1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y + 1}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{4y + 1} \\\\\n\\sqrt{4y + 1} &= 5x \\\\\n(\\sqrt{4y + 1})^{2} &= (5x)^{2} \\\\\n4y + 1 &= 25x^{2} \\\\\n4y &= 25x^{2} - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{4}(25x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(25x^2 - 1\\right)\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq -\\dfrac14, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq -\\dfrac14\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10be7535-989f-5706-964e-06c02251d1f5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8760afc0-a7fb-5398-a585-c5559c9d1bb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10458" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = 3\\sqrt{x + k} - 2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 + 3$. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x+k} - 2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y + k} - 2\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 3\\sqrt{y + k} - 2 \\\\\n3\\sqrt{y + k} &= x + 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{y + k} &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 2) \\\\\n(\\sqrt{y + k})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{3}(x + 2))^{2} \\\\\ny + k &= \\frac{1}{9}(x + 2)^{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{9}(x + 2)^{2} - k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x + 2)^2 - k\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k = 3 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = -3\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3, \\qquad f(x) \\geq -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f689c3e3-9bb9-5e1e-b69a-b691373fdee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10496" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}} + 1$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}} + 1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} + 1 \\\\\nx - 1 &= \\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x - 1} &= \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{3} \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{y} &= \\frac{3}{x - 1} \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} &= (\\frac{3}{x - 1})^{3} \\\\\ny &= (27)/((x - 1)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{27}{(x - 1)^3}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\neq 0, \\qquad f(x)\\neq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\neq 1, \\qquad f(x)\\neq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192707 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe915c05-2906-5873-8fd5-f364816f83bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b450035f-a3f0-53e5-8c79-93d847a42ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{-1}(x) = 9(x + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189893} {:answer/id #uuid "e3729da8-3cd3-5201-ad4f-57655be8f6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{9}{x^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189894} {:answer/id #uuid "10920625-3b2b-5a4b-8959-a88b28e0be45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 